#!/usr/bin/env bash
# run2.sh <run> : Bolt art trial v2, portrait style. Needs $R/reference/{frames,portrait}.png
T='D:/Coding/BYMR/art-trials/bolt/kit'
R="$T/$1"
date +%T
bash ~/.claude/scripts/agy-delegate.sh --write --yolo --timeout 10m --dir "$R" -- "You are repainting tiny game sprites. Workspace folder: $R. reference/portrait.png is the OFFICIAL concept painting of the monster (the Bolt): a red/flesh-coloured fanged worm with cracked textured skin, a dark black-blue spiked segmented shell on its back with red thorn spikes and small red legs, a ring-shaped mouth full of sharp fangs with a red tongue, and two glowing purple eye bulbs on the sides of its head. reference/frames.png is a 2x2 grid (cells 320x256 px, grey ground) of 4 low-resolution isometric game sprite frames of the same monster blown up 8x (old design, blurry): top-left facing right/east, top-right facing down/south toward the viewer, bottom-left facing left/west, bottom-right facing up/north showing its back. TASK: generate ONE new image saved as out/frames-new.png with exactly the same 2x2 grid layout and canvas proportions as reference/frames.png (640x512 or a 2x multiple); each monster at the same position, same size, same pose and same facing as the matching reference cell, BUT redesigned to look exactly like the monster in portrait.png (use the portrait for design, colours, anatomy and painting style; use frames.png only for pose, facing, position and size, ignore its blue/indigo colours). Style: the same hand-painted digital illustration look as the portrait: soft shading, rich texture, gritty detail, glossy highlights on eyes and shell, NO thick cartoon outline, NOT cute, no cel shading, no pixel art. Seen from the isometric 3/4 game camera at each facing (south: face toward viewer; north: show the spiked shell back and tail end). Keep the design identical in all 4 cells. Each cell: the monster on a soft dark ground shadow (blurred ellipse under it) like in the reference. Background everywhere (including the whole cell outside the shadow) must be flat pure magenta #FF00FF, no grid lines, no text, no labels. IMPORTANT: do NOT write or run any code or scripts, and do NOT warp, cut, copy or composite pixels from any reference image - the new image must be produced by ONE call to your image generation tool, passing both reference images as inputs. Then save it as out/frames-new.png. Reply DONE when the file exists." 2>&1 | tail -4
date +%T; ls -la "$R/out"
